// steadyPowerLawRheology: a non-Newtonian fill in the steady network, one
// generalized Reynolds number away from the Newtonian law.
//
// Both ends are fixed-pressure nodes, so every pipe carries exactly
// dp = p(A) - p(B) and its rate is the closed form of that drop -- no network
// solve stands between the assertion and the physics, and the eight pipes are
// eight independent statements in one run.
//
// The kernel change is one substitution: where the Darcy-Weisbach law asked for
// Re = rho u d / mu it now asks for the Metzner-Reed number
//
//     Re_MR = rho u^(2-n) d^n / (K' 8^(n-1)),   K' = K (3n+1)/(4n)
//
// in the same Churchill call. That is the whole rheology: tau = K gdot^n. Its
// payoff is that Churchill's laminar branch, f = 64/Re, is then *not* a
// correlation for this fluid but its exact solution,
//
//     dp = (L/d) K (3n+1)/n (8u/d)^n   ->   Q = (pi d^3/32) [n dp d/((3n+1) K L)]^(1/n)
//
// which at n = 1, K = mu is 128 mu L Q = pi d^4 dp. So the assertions below are
// against a closed form, and three structural exponents come out of it:
//
//   * dp      -> Q ~ dp^(1/n)        (Newtonian 1/2: exponent 1 vs 2)
//   * d       -> Q ~ d^((3n+1)/n)    (Newtonian 4;  n=0.5: 5, i.e. 32x per doubling)
//   * L       -> Q ~ 1/L            (unchanged -- the rheology moves the slope
//                                    in dp and in d, never the length exponent)
//
// and one equivalence is the inertness control: n = 1 with K = mu is the
// Newtonian number term for term, so `PowN1` must be *bit-identical* to the
// undeclared `Newt`, and every undeclared pipe in the other 98 tests must not
// move at all.
//
// Shear-thinning (n < 1) buys flow cheaply at a rising drop; shear-thickening
// (n > 1) is the opposite. Both are in one run so the two signs of the same
// exponent are read against each other rather than against a remembered number.
//
// Re_MR stays below ~170 everywhere, including the doubled-dp file, so the
// laminar branch is the one in force and the closed form is exact rather than
// approximate. The turbulent half of Churchill is *reached* by the generalized
// number but is not validated against anything here -- that is this
// increment's boundary, not a claim.
//
// verified by tests/check_steady_power_law_rheology.py
// (ctest verify.steadyPowerLawRheology).

fluid
{
    rho 850; mu 0.1;
}

nodes
{
    // 1.0e4 Pa across every pipe. case_dpDouble.dict raises the upstream node
    // to 4.0e4, which doubles the drop and nothing else.
    A { pressure 3.0e4; }
    B { pressure 2.0e4; }
}

pipes
{
    // --- the equivalence control pair ---
    Newt  { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.02; }          // undeclared
    PowN1 { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.02;
            flowIndex 1; consistency 0.1; }                    // must be bit-identical

    // --- the sign of the exponent, same geometry ---
    Thin05  { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.02;
              flowIndex 0.5; consistency 1.0; }
    Thin07  { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.02;
              flowIndex 0.7; consistency 2.0; }
    Thick13 { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.02;
              flowIndex 1.3; consistency 0.02; }

    // --- the diameter exponent: 4 for Newtonian, (3n+1)/n = 5 at n = 0.5 ---
    D2xNewt { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.04; }
    D2xThin { from A; to B; length 20; diameter 0.04;
              flowIndex 0.5; consistency 1.0; }

    // --- the length exponent is not moved by the rheology ---
    LongNewt { from A; to B; length 40; diameter 0.02; }
    LongThin { from A; to B; length 40; diameter 0.02;
               flowIndex 0.5; consistency 1.0; }
}

solve { maxIter 60; tol 1e-12; }
